## Why This Is Asked

Tests a robust, branchless 128-bit rotation, ensuring correct cross-boundary behavior and emphasis on manipulating split 64-bit halves.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit rotation across hi/lo halves
- Branchless implementation via masks and shifts
- Edge cases: n = 0, n = 64, n = 127

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
  n %= 128;
  uint128 res;
  if (n == 0) return x; // illustrative intent; prefer mask-based approach to avoid branches
  // Branchless approach would compute both paths and select via masks
  if (n < 64) {
    res.lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    res.hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
  } else {
    unsigned m = n - 64;
    res.lo = (x.hi << m) | (x.lo >> (64 - m));
    res.hi = (x.lo << m) | (x.hi >> (64 - m));
  }
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like n=0, n=64, n=127? 
- How would you adapt this for a 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes low-level bit-tracking across a 128-bit value, edge cases at zero, and portable handling when single-word intrinsics are unavailable. It mirrors real-world tasks where bitmaps or flags span multiple words.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit split into two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count logic with early non-zero word optimizations
- Portable fallback for environments lacking 64-bit ctzn intrinsics
- Edge case for zero input

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  if (x.lo) return __builtin_ctzll(x.lo);
  if (x.hi) return 64 + __builtin_ctzll(x.hi);
  return 128;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
